1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,455
The job cloned the repository.
The clone succeeded.

2
00:00:07,495 --> 00:00:12,329
git status in the job's checkout is clean.
The model file is where the code expects

3
00:00:12,369 --> 00:00:17,203
it, with the right name. And the loader
fails with a parse error on the first bytes

4
00:00:17,243 --> 00:00:17,908
of the file.

5
00:00:18,248 --> 00:00:22,190
Open the file and you can
read it: three lines of text.

6
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:26,389
A version URL, a line that
begins oid sha256:, and a size.

7
00:00:26,429 --> 00:00:31,241
It's a ticket for a coat, on a
machine with no cloakroom attendant.

8
00:00:31,581 --> 00:00:36,490
Nothing is wrong from Git's point of view.
That's what makes this failure slow to

9
00:00:36,530 --> 00:00:40,707
diagnose the first time, and a
ten-second diagnosis every time after.

10
00:00:40,747 --> 00:00:44,006
By the end of this video,
those ten seconds are yours.

11
00:00:45,846 --> 00:00:49,881
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:49,921 --> 00:00:55,493
A reminder: Git LFS keeps large files outside
Git and leaves a small pointer file in

13
00:00:55,533 --> 00:01:00,904
their place. In the last video you saw the
two stores on one machine: Git objects

14
00:01:00,944 --> 00:01:05,246
with a small pointer blob, and the
LFS store with the real bytes.

15
00:01:05,286 --> 00:01:08,987
Today the two stores travel,
and they travel separately.

16
00:01:09,027 --> 00:01:12,328
Git objects by Git. LFS
objects by the LFS client.

17
00:01:12,668 --> 00:01:16,117
You'll watch a push put content
into a remote's LFS store.

18
00:01:16,157 --> 00:01:19,787
Then a clone on a machine without
the client, and the repair.

19
00:01:19,827 --> 00:01:24,840
Then downloads on demand and how to skip
them. And last the local store, which grows

20
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:29,893
with every version until you prune it, with
one warning about what the reflog, Git's

21
00:01:29,933 --> 00:01:33,743
local journal of where your branches
have been, doesn't protect.

22
00:01:34,083 --> 00:01:38,876
One thing to know before the first replay:
the push demonstration is a volatile

23
00:01:38,916 --> 00:01:43,893
script. Its transcript isn't compared byte
for byte with a fresh run, for a reason

24
00:01:43,933 --> 00:01:44,566
you'll see.

25
00:01:46,406 --> 00:01:49,707
read git lfs status and say
what a push will upload;

26
00:01:50,047 --> 00:01:54,320
say where LFS objects go on push
and how the endpoint is derived;

27
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:59,478
recognize a clone made without the client
by the size of its files, and repair it;

28
00:01:59,818 --> 00:02:02,823
fetch on demand and skip the
smudge filter for speed;

29
00:02:03,163 --> 00:02:07,611
explain how git lfs prune decides what
to delete and when that is unsafe.

30
00:02:09,451 --> 00:02:14,318
Day to day. git status reports a modified
LFS file as usual: Git ran the clean

31
00:02:14,358 --> 00:02:19,477
filter on the working tree file, got a
different pointer, and compared it with the

32
00:02:19,517 --> 00:02:24,573
index. git diff shows the change between
the two pointers, which tells a reviewer

33
00:02:24,613 --> 00:02:29,418
that the file changed and by how much
it grew, and nothing about its content.

34
00:02:29,458 --> 00:02:34,703
🟢 SAFE: git lfs status adds the LFS view:
which objects a push would upload, and for

35
00:02:34,743 --> 00:02:38,855
changed files whether the index
and working tree hold LFS content.

36
00:02:39,195 --> 00:02:44,421
The hooks. git lfs install wrote four hooks.
pre-push is the important one: git lfs

37
00:02:44,461 --> 00:02:49,370
pre-push uploads the LFS objects referenced
by the commits about to be pushed,

38
00:02:49,410 --> 00:02:53,748
before Git sends the commits. The
other three exist for file locking.

39
00:02:53,788 --> 00:02:59,014
Two consequences of the upload living in a
hook, both of which you can predict from

40
00:02:59,054 --> 00:03:03,519
the hooks videos: git push --no-verify
skips it, and a repository whose

41
00:03:03,559 --> 00:03:08,595
core.hooksPath points elsewhere doesn't
run it unless the LFS hook was installed

42
00:03:08,635 --> 00:03:08,976
there.

43
00:03:09,316 --> 00:03:15,390
Where the objects go. The LFS client
derives its server from the Git remote.

44
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:21,987
For an HTTPS or SSH remote it appends
/info/lfs to the repository URL and talks to

45
00:03:22,027 --> 00:03:28,665
an HTTP API there. lfs.url, or a committed
.lfsconfig file, can point it elsewhere.

46
00:03:28,705 --> 00:03:34,779
For the lab's path remote it resolves
a file:// endpoint and uses a built-in

47
00:03:34,819 --> 00:03:41,376
standalone file adapter, which copies
objects into an lfs/objects directory inside

48
00:03:41,416 --> 00:03:42,985
the bare repository.

49
00:03:43,325 --> 00:03:47,295
Cloning without the client.
.gitattributes names a filter called lfs.

50
00:03:47,335 --> 00:03:51,770
On a machine where no such filter is
defined, an undefined filter is a no-op.

51
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:56,419
The clone succeeds, git status is clean,
and the file in the working tree is the

52
00:03:56,459 --> 00:04:01,068
pointer. The textbook quotes GitHub's
documentation on collaborators without the

53
00:04:01,108 --> 00:04:05,949
client: they "will only fetch the pointer
files, and won't have access to any of the

54
00:04:05,989 --> 00:04:06,705
actual data".

55
00:04:07,045 --> 00:04:12,172
The repair, in two halves. 🟢 SAFE: git
lfs fetch downloads the objects that the

56
00:04:12,212 --> 00:04:17,667
current commit needs into the local store.
🟢 SAFE: git lfs checkout replaces pointer

57
00:04:17,707 --> 00:04:23,096
files in the working tree with content from
the local store, and never overwrites a

58
00:04:23,136 --> 00:04:26,431
modified file, so nothing
is lost and no ref moves.

59
00:04:26,471 --> 00:04:28,590
git lfs pull is the two together.

60
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:33,678
On a machine where the filter is configured
before the clone, none of these commands

61
00:04:33,718 --> 00:04:38,123
is needed. The smudge filter, the step
that swaps a pointer for the real file,

62
00:04:38,163 --> 00:04:42,569
downloads each object as the checkout
writes each file, and again on any later

63
00:04:42,609 --> 00:04:45,818
checkout that needs a version
not yet in the local store.

64
00:04:46,158 --> 00:04:52,166
Skipping. With the environment variable
GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE set to 1, the smudge

65
00:04:52,206 --> 00:04:58,365
filter passes pointers through untouched.
git lfs install --skip-smudge makes this

66
00:04:58,405 --> 00:05:04,186
the permanent behavior of a configuration,
and git lfs pull --include, or the

67
00:05:04,226 --> 00:05:08,949
settings lfs.fetchinclude and
lfs.fetchexclude, fetch a subset.

68
00:05:09,289 --> 00:05:14,112
The local store. Every version you
have added or checked out stays in

69
00:05:14,152 --> 00:05:19,820
.git/lfs/objects. git gc doesn't touch it.
🟡 CAUTION: git lfs prune deletes local

70
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:25,669
objects that the current checkout doesn't
need, that aren't "recent", and that have

71
00:05:25,709 --> 00:05:31,448
been pushed. --dry-run --verbose previews.
--verify-remote asks the remote whether

72
00:05:31,488 --> 00:05:35,465
it really has each object
before deleting the local copy.

73
00:05:35,805 --> 00:05:41,155
"Recent" is defined by settings such as
lfs.fetchrecentrefsdays, 7 days, plus

74
00:05:41,195 --> 00:05:45,424
lfs.pruneoffsetdays, 3 days,
measured against the real clock.

75
00:05:45,464 --> 00:05:50,814
The lab's commits are dated 7 September
2026, so in the replay they are never

76
00:05:50,854 --> 00:05:56,414
recent. In a repository with fresh commits,
prune keeps more than the transcript

77
00:05:56,454 --> 00:05:57,044
suggests.

78
00:05:57,384 --> 00:06:02,012
And the warning. The help text of git
lfs prune states: "The reflog is not

79
00:06:02,052 --> 00:06:07,311
considered, only commits. Therefore LFS
objects that are only referenced by orphaned

80
00:06:07,351 --> 00:06:12,610
commits are always deleted." A model version
that only an abandoned commit refers to

81
00:06:12,650 --> 00:06:15,322
isn't protected by Git's
reflog safety net.

82
00:06:17,162 --> 00:06:22,029
Keep the coat check. Today there are two
buildings, your laptop and the server, and

83
00:06:22,069 --> 00:06:24,335
each has a ticket desk and a cloakroom.

84
00:06:24,675 --> 00:06:28,732
Tickets move between the ticket desks
whenever Git pushes or fetches.

85
00:06:28,772 --> 00:06:33,006
Coats move between the cloakrooms only
when the LFS client carries them.

86
00:06:33,046 --> 00:06:37,637
On a push, the client carries the coats
first and Git sends the tickets after.

87
00:06:37,677 --> 00:06:42,386
On a checkout, the smudge filter reads a
ticket and fetches that one coat if the

88
00:06:42,426 --> 00:06:44,286
local cloakroom doesn't have it.

89
00:06:44,626 --> 00:06:48,691
A machine without the client has a
ticket desk and no cloakroom staff.

90
00:06:48,731 --> 00:06:52,210
It receives every ticket and no
coat, and nothing complains.

91
00:06:52,550 --> 00:06:57,259
The analogy needs one correction for pruning.
A cloakroom clears out coats that are

92
00:06:57,299 --> 00:07:00,750
also stored at the other building
and that nobody is wearing.

93
00:07:00,790 --> 00:07:05,556
It doesn't look at your old ticket stubs in
the wastepaper basket: commits that only

94
00:07:05,596 --> 00:07:08,818
the reflog remembers don't
count as "someone needs this".

95
00:07:10,658 --> 00:07:15,699
The top pair of arrows is Git. The bottom
pair is the LFS client: the pre-push hook

96
00:07:15,739 --> 00:07:19,739
on the way up, the smudge filter
or git lfs fetch on the way down.

97
00:07:19,779 --> 00:07:23,106
The last line of the diagram
is the hook of this video.

98
00:07:27,446 --> 00:07:33,578
The endpoint is derived from the remote:
here a file:// URL, with the transfer

99
00:07:33,618 --> 00:07:40,146
adapter lfs-standalone-file in the list.
For an HTTPS or SSH remote it would be the

100
00:07:40,186 --> 00:07:43,232
repository URL with /info/lfs appended.

101
00:07:43,572 --> 00:07:48,917
The line "Uploading LFS objects" is printed
by the pre-push hook, before Git's own

102
00:07:48,957 --> 00:07:54,236
output. The "0 B" is an artifact of the
file adapter, which doesn't report bytes.

103
00:07:54,576 --> 00:08:00,539
The remote now has two stores, like your
clone. Git objects, with a 132-byte blob

104
00:08:00,579 --> 00:08:06,765
for the path. And an LFS directory with one
full file per version, named by SHA-256.

105
00:08:07,105 --> 00:08:13,342
One thing a file remote can't do.
Replay labs/run ch22/lfs-lock.

106
00:08:13,682 --> 00:08:17,009
Locking is a server feature
and wasn't run for the book.

107
00:08:17,049 --> 00:08:21,939
It exists because binary files can't be
merged: two people who edit the same model

108
00:08:21,979 --> 00:08:25,607
in parallel produce a conflict
that only one of them can win.

109
00:08:25,947 --> 00:08:30,773
Predict three things about this clone:
whether it succeeds, what git status says,

110
00:08:30,813 --> 00:08:33,717
and how many bytes the
model file has. I'll wait.

111
00:08:38,557 --> 00:08:43,863
The clone succeeds. The model file is the
pointer: three lines of text, 132 bytes,

112
00:08:43,903 --> 00:08:49,339
where the model should be. git status is
clean. If you expected an error, that's the

113
00:08:49,379 --> 00:08:54,033
natural guess, and it's exactly why
this failure is slow the first time.

114
00:08:54,373 --> 00:08:59,893
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. The
broken CI job from the opening is in front

115
00:08:59,933 --> 00:09:03,599
of you. Write down the first
two commands you would run.

116
00:09:03,639 --> 00:09:04,261
I'll wait.

117
00:09:09,101 --> 00:09:13,830
Here's the textbook's answer. Look at
the first two hundred bytes of the file.

118
00:09:13,870 --> 00:09:17,314
Then ask git lfs ls-files which
paths are still pointers.

119
00:09:17,354 --> 00:09:20,310
That's the ten-second
diagnosis from the opening.

120
00:09:20,650 --> 00:09:26,402
A minus sign. And look at what git lfs pull
did: it downloaded the object into the

121
00:09:26,442 --> 00:09:32,265
local store, and then declined to touch the
working tree, "Git LFS is not installed

122
00:09:32,305 --> 00:09:36,362
for this repository". Now the
repair, one piece at a time.

123
00:09:36,402 --> 00:09:42,296
A quick quiz first: after git lfs fetch
alone, is the model file still 132 bytes, or

124
00:09:42,336 --> 00:09:45,051
already its real size? Say it out loud.

125
00:09:49,891 --> 00:09:51,788
Still 132 bytes.

126
00:09:52,128 --> 00:09:56,120
After fetch, the object is in the
store and the file hasn't changed.

127
00:09:56,160 --> 00:10:00,448
After checkout, the file has its real
size and the marker is an asterisk.

128
00:10:00,788 --> 00:10:05,069
With the filter configured, a checkout
that needs another version downloads it.

129
00:10:05,409 --> 00:10:13,182
The older version, 0269885262, arrived in
the local store at the moment the checkout

130
00:10:13,222 --> 00:10:16,344
needed it. Now skip it on purpose.

131
00:10:23,884 --> 00:10:28,836
A pointer in the working tree, a minus in
the listing, and a clean status: the clean

132
00:10:28,876 --> 00:10:33,828
filter recognises a pointer and passes it
through unchanged, so the file matches the

133
00:10:33,868 --> 00:10:38,760
index. This is how a job that doesn't need
the large files avoids downloading them.

134
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:42,961
One note on GitHub Actions,
which was not run for the book.

135
00:10:43,001 --> 00:10:48,183
The textbook states that actions/checkout
downloads LFS files only when its lfs

136
00:10:48,223 --> 00:10:53,670
input is true, and that the default is
false. A job with the default therefore sees

137
00:10:53,710 --> 00:10:59,091
pointers. A job with lfs: true downloads
objects, and GitHub's billing page counts

138
00:10:59,131 --> 00:11:00,281
that as bandwidth.

139
00:11:00,621 --> 00:11:03,488
Two versions in the store,
and one is checked out.

140
00:11:03,828 --> 00:11:08,363
Two local objects, one retained, one
verified with the remote and deleted.

141
00:11:08,403 --> 00:11:11,825
A pruned object isn't lost as
long as the remote has it.

142
00:11:12,165 --> 00:11:18,057
git lfs fetch --all downloads every object
referenced by any commit, which is what a

143
00:11:18,097 --> 00:11:23,989
backup or a migration to another host needs.
git lfs fsck verifies that the files in

144
00:11:24,029 --> 00:11:26,460
the local store match their hashes.

145
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:31,648
Now two more versions, committed and
not pushed. Predict what prune deletes.

146
00:11:31,688 --> 00:11:33,384
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

147
00:11:38,224 --> 00:11:42,544
The two pushed versions were deleted. The
two unpushed ones stayed, including the

148
00:11:42,584 --> 00:11:43,997
one that isn't checked out.

149
00:11:44,337 --> 00:11:49,406
prune refuses to delete what exists only
on your machine, as long as a commit on a

150
00:11:49,446 --> 00:11:54,203
ref refers to it. Remember the exception
from the help text: the reflog isn't

151
00:11:54,243 --> 00:11:54,889
considered.

152
00:11:56,729 --> 00:11:58,377
Five mistakes to watch for.

153
00:11:58,717 --> 00:12:02,695
Debugging the model loader
when the file is 132 bytes.

154
00:12:02,735 --> 00:12:08,723
Root cause: the checkout ran without an
LFS filter, so the working tree holds the

155
00:12:08,763 --> 00:12:13,039
pointer; head -c 200 and git
lfs ls-files show it at once.

156
00:12:13,379 --> 00:12:19,221
git push --no-verify in an LFS repository.
Root cause: the upload of LFS objects is

157
00:12:19,261 --> 00:12:25,103
done by the pre-push hook, which that option
skips; the pointers go and the content

158
00:12:25,143 --> 00:12:26,025
stays behind.

159
00:12:26,365 --> 00:12:30,586
Expecting git lfs pull to fix a
clone that has no filter configured.

160
00:12:30,626 --> 00:12:35,849
Root cause: it downloads into the local
store and declines to touch the working tree

161
00:12:35,889 --> 00:12:38,982
until git lfs install has
run for that repository.

162
00:12:39,322 --> 00:12:45,265
Treating git gc as cleanup for model versions.
Root cause: the LFS store is not part

163
00:12:45,305 --> 00:12:49,467
of the object database; only
git lfs prune removes from it.

164
00:12:49,807 --> 00:12:54,903
Pruning after a reset or an amend and
expecting the reflog to save the old model

165
00:12:54,943 --> 00:12:59,653
version. Root cause: git lfs prune
considers commits on refs only; objects

166
00:12:59,693 --> 00:13:03,312
referenced solely by orphaned
commits are always deleted.

167
00:13:05,152 --> 00:13:09,263
Now, out of the lab. A speech
team's CI has two kinds of job.

168
00:13:09,303 --> 00:13:13,210
The lint and unit-test jobs
don't load the acoustic model.

169
00:13:13,250 --> 00:13:18,790
They run with the default checkout and see
pointers, which costs no LFS bandwidth.

170
00:13:18,830 --> 00:13:23,825
The evaluation job loads the model,
and its checkout asks for LFS content.

171
00:13:24,165 --> 00:13:28,577
One week the evaluation job fails with
a parse error in the model loader.

172
00:13:28,617 --> 00:13:33,456
The engineer on call runs two commands in
the job's workspace before reading any

173
00:13:33,496 --> 00:13:38,090
Python: the first two hundred bytes of
the model file, and git lfs ls-files.

174
00:13:38,130 --> 00:13:42,664
The file starts with the word "version"
and the listing shows a minus sign.

175
00:13:43,004 --> 00:13:47,774
Somebody had copied the lint job's
checkout step into the evaluation job.

176
00:13:47,814 --> 00:13:53,111
The fix is one line in the workflow. The
team then adds a guard to the evaluation

177
00:13:53,151 --> 00:13:58,383
script itself: it refuses to start if the
model file begins with the LFS version

178
00:13:58,423 --> 00:13:59,635
line, and says why.

179
00:14:01,475 --> 00:14:08,863
Your turn. Do Exercise 15.8, Level
3, "A model file of 131 bytes", in

180
00:14:08,903 --> 00:14:13,599
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

181
00:14:13,939 --> 00:14:19,078
Before you run a diagnostic, write down
what you expect each of these to print in

182
00:14:19,118 --> 00:14:24,384
the broken checkout: the first bytes of the
file, git status, git lfs ls-files, and

183
00:14:24,424 --> 00:14:29,626
git config get filter.lfs.smudge. Then
repair it in two separate steps and predict

184
00:14:29,666 --> 00:14:31,224
the file size after each.

185
00:14:31,564 --> 00:14:36,984
The challenge is Exercise 15.9, Level 4,
"It works on your machine", in the same

186
00:14:37,024 --> 00:14:37,325
file.

187
00:14:41,165 --> 00:14:46,444
Answer out loud. A strong answer names what
the 132 bytes are and explains, from how

188
00:14:46,484 --> 00:14:51,573
filters behave when undefined, why the
clone succeeded and nothing warned anyone.

189
00:14:51,613 --> 00:14:56,639
The two diagnostics are cheap, read-only,
and each says what output confirms the

190
00:14:56,679 --> 00:15:01,768
cause. The fix names the layer it belongs
to, GitHub Actions and not Git, and the

191
00:15:01,808 --> 00:15:06,454
specific input of the checkout step,
and it mentions the cost that the fix

192
00:15:06,494 --> 00:15:10,317
introduces and how jobs that
don't need the content avoid it.

193
00:15:10,657 --> 00:15:15,112
A complete answer also proposes a guard
so that the next occurrence fails with a

194
00:15:15,152 --> 00:15:15,898
clear message.

195
00:15:17,738 --> 00:15:23,138
Let's land this. One picture to keep: when
no smudge filter runs, the working tree,

196
00:15:23,178 --> 00:15:28,447
the index and the commit all hold the same
pointer, so Git reports nothing wrong.

197
00:15:28,787 --> 00:15:30,447
You should now be able to say:

198
00:15:30,787 --> 00:15:35,646
git lfs status shows what a push will
upload; the upload is done by the pre-push

199
00:15:35,686 --> 00:15:37,728
hook before Git sends the commits.

200
00:15:38,068 --> 00:15:43,439
The LFS endpoint is derived from the Git
remote, and the remote has two stores as my

201
00:15:43,479 --> 00:15:44,148
clone does.

202
00:15:44,488 --> 00:15:49,871
A checkout without the LFS filter holds
pointer files, and Git reports nothing

203
00:15:49,911 --> 00:15:53,208
wrong; git lfs ls-files
marks them with a minus.

204
00:15:53,548 --> 00:15:59,565
git lfs fetch fills the local store and
git lfs checkout replaces pointers;

205
00:15:59,605 --> 00:16:02,634
GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE=1 skips downloads.

206
00:16:02,974 --> 00:16:08,015
git lfs prune deletes pushed, non-recent
